Step 1 — Create Project
Open Visual Studio 2019.
1. Create a new project.
2. Select ASP.NET Web Application (.NET Framework).
3. Project name: StudentMVC
4. Select .NET Framework 4.7.2.
5. Select MVC template.
6. Authentication: No Authentication.
7. Click Create.     Practical9
Step 2 — Modify HomeController
Open:
Controllers → HomeController.cs
Add this method inside HomeController:
public ActionResult Student()
{
    ViewBag.Name = "Rahul";
    ViewBag.Course = "B.Sc. Information Technology";
    ViewBag.RollNo = 101;
    return View();
}

The Controller sends Name, Course and Roll Number to the View using ViewBag.     Practical9
Step 3 — Create Student View
Right-click inside the Student() method → Add View
Enter:
- View name: Student
- Template: Empty (without model)
Click Add.
It creates:
Views → Home → Student.cshtml     Practical9
Step 4 — Add View Code
Open:
Views/Home/Student.cshtml
Replace the code with:
@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Student Information";
}

<h2>Student Information</h2>

<p><strong>Name:</strong> @ViewBag.Name</p>
<p><strong>Roll Number:</strong> @ViewBag.RollNo</p>
<p><strong>Course:</strong> @ViewBag.Course</p>

Step 5 — Run
Press Ctrl + F5.
Open:
/Home/Student

Example:
https://localhost:xxxxx/Home/Student

Output
Student Information

Name: Rahul
Roll Number: 101
Course: B.Sc. Information Technology

The PDF explains that /Home/Student maps to:
HomeController → Student() → Views/Home/Student.cshtml.     Practical9
Part 6 — Create Second Controller
Right-click:
Controllers → Add → Controller
Select:
MVC 5 Controller - Empty
Name:
StudentController
Click Add.     Practical9
Replace the code with:
using System.Web.Mvc;

namespace StudentMVC.Controllers
{
    public class StudentController : Controller
    {
        public ActionResult Index()
        {
            ViewBag.Message = "Welcome to Student Management";
            return View();
        }

        public ActionResult Details()
        {
            ViewBag.Name = "Priya";
            ViewBag.Course = "B.Sc. Information Technology";
            ViewBag.RollNo = 102;
            return View();
        }
    }
}

Step 7 — Create Details View
Right-click inside:
public ActionResult Details()

Select Add View.
- View name: Details
- Template: Empty (without model)
Click Add.
Open:
Views/Student/Details.cshtml
Add:
@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Student Details";
}

<h2>Student Details</h2>

<p>Name: @ViewBag.Name</p>
<p>Roll Number: @ViewBag.RollNo</p>
<p>Course: @ViewBag.Course</p>

    Practical9
Step 8 — Run Second Controller
Run the application and open:
/Student/Details

Output
Student Details

Name: Priya
Roll Number: 102
Course: B.Sc. Information Technology
